# Why: THREE dependencies still pin a React 18 peer range against this app's React 19, so a plain
# `npm install` fails ERESOLVE on a clean clone — cmdk@1.0.0 (peer react ^18.0.0),
# next-themes@0.3.0 (^16.8 || ^17 || ^18) and sonner@1.5.0 (^18.0.0). All three, not one: bumping
# cmdk alone does not resolve, it just moves the error to next-themes. cmdk is merely the first
# name npm happens to print. Note this is NOT about the Vitest peer conflict whose incidental
# mention of this flag blamed a @typescript-eslint clash that no longer exists.
#
# MUST live in this directory, not the repo root: npm reads the per-project .npmrc from the PACKAGE
# root only, and a root-level copy reads as `false` — it would look like the fix, pass review, and
# leave the next cloner hitting the same ERESOLVE.
#
# EXIT CONDITION — delete this file when all three are on their React-19 lines (cmdk@1.1.1,
# next-themes@0.4.6, sonner@2.0.7). It is not free while it stays: legacy-peer-deps=true changes
# resolution for every install forever and makes `npm ci` skip peer validation, so it trades one
# loud bounded failure for a quiet unbounded one — the peer range is the only automated signal that
# a dependency has not been checked against a React major, and nothing detects that loss.
legacy-peer-deps=true
